063be8ede925099a9dd47ba598ca4fab8190c4f7c4889595.svg)\n\nFirst, notice that ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) is a type I region with representation\n![](images/66/668bf889423235cf4ad8497796bfd3e50140d258a325a75b84e4a6822b288204.svg)\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/c1/c1301c17b1dbb8b48716d09ad6f0a7a105917ea7a1c0e942bbe087b12772f254.png)\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as \n![](images/41/4115a69fc623e7ccfc7cfccb7285afc0439a111ae4f2424693481fbf7dcd3c54.svg)\nwhere ![](images/8d/8dc6f7e80ecaac9fa07890ce1881dd51de319597ae8b1dce821aa03c1f3eff06.svg) is a constant of proportionality. \n\nSince the density contains the unknown constant ![](images/8d/8dc6f7e80ecaac9fa07890ce1881dd51de319597ae8b1dce821aa03c1f3eff06.svg), we first use the total mass ![](images/34/34058a2a5d1de088feed7d9825ba218394c9a8bb880b046be7b47307cdd37ce1.svg) to determine ![](images/c7/c7432bf7a520e569e53fdd1a7303ce6b9f940d99e68b5e7ea3196c51acfe2b69.svg)\n\nWe can find an expression for ![](images/8d/8dc6f7e80ecaac9fa07890ce1881dd51de319597ae8b1dce821aa03c1f3eff06.svg) in terms of ![](images/34/34058a2a5d1de088feed7d9825ba218394c9a8bb880b046be7b47307cdd37ce1.svg) by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n![](images/03/03176dbe18e8d06ce05d43951fd0759d88ab36e651d08de70ce17465da4e9128.svg)\n\nTherefore, ![](images/55/55f2ccc4062bb6cdf9af22e7f89a1a17bf6445718da120e253149a8a314616f3.svg) and hence ![](images/38/3895193c54afc999ca98d66da9ff11ad1747545f8d786e6e1e8630aaf74cfffa.svg) Therefore, the moment of inertia about the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis is\n![](images/e7/e7f8cc88a82c9fec7d20bcf57129dea1d569e1a19ef9bcbe9e6be47e9841d939.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128863 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12483" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128863 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/12/12fb5f07885b074f983cab3a5fba0faff607d8aa34d7cd3abe4e94abf53dac25.png)\n\nConsider the plate that occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the polar circles $r = 1$ and $r = 2$ and the polar rays $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. The mass density function of the plate is given by $\\lambda(x,y) = \\dfrac{1}{x^2}.$ Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128863 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dbf6a3b7-abb9-5f62-83af-00169c673bc6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128863 #edb/ref 1074 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda(r,\\theta),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\nI_x = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda(r,\\theta) \\cdot r^3\\sin^2{\\theta} \\:\\textrm{d}r\\:\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\nI_y = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda(r,\\theta) \\cdot  r^3\\cos^2{\\theta} \\:\\textrm{d}r\\:\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\nI_0 = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda(r,\\theta) \\cdot  r^3 \\:\\textrm{d}r\\:\\textrm{d}\\theta = I_x + I_y.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that the region $D$ in polar coordinates can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 1 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\:\\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nAlso, the mass density function in polar coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\lambda(r,\\theta) = \\dfrac{1}{r^2 \\cos^2{\\theta}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =({\\int }_{1}^{2}r\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\,{|}_{1}^{2}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4-1)\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129030 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8542" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129030 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/de/de882e240d0a6ca824e77397fe9c6b51eb94d36c2e5e3524422ef0a0f28103de.png)\n\nA triangular plate of mass ![](images/34/34058a2a5d1de088feed7d9825ba218394c9a8bb880b046be7b47307cdd37ce1.svg) and uniform mass density occupies the region ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) enclosed between the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis and the lines ![](images/b3/b3259a74b1b63f2b49841acd0eea4f599e9dacf9c4f1b17bda0a3cb115a9d90e.svg) and ![](images/fd/fdad9cd601a0652f1c927ea8e805525f51c3a144e924e6eabe5c75d186e3062b.svg) as shown above. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129030 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c317696a-899d-5af5-9bf4-2d38862f5877" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129030 #edb/ref 1074 "For a plane lamina ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) with mass density function ![](images/ac/ac8c3fda52a02808979c7543290abb75797c85b3b0db8aed7a51cc2afda66995.svg) we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis is given by\n![](images/66/66764283bb64f05a967263265ff2c3253049505060577b19b8beb83be225efb9.svg)\n\n- The moment of inertia about the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis is given by\n![](images/f3/f30602db401e65f6fa3e007153a1e2d8ddd35f5ef202bab4118ba2663ec03325.svg)\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n![](images/a6/a6f586b23532d418063be8ede925099a9dd47ba598ca4fab8190c4f7c4889595.svg)\n\nFirst, notice that ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) is a type I region with representation\n\n![](images/0b/0b668ee2254c04000cdd2a7f9434e780844366cd2f6f4dbd0798840a688d744e.svg)\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/68/68904cbee1839a61f4d65c5ff03066d8f28b8a5e070f6ddb00c82c29cfd93fcd.png)\n\nThe area of the triangular plate ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) is \n![](images/7f/7fa087c9262341fe08582d9053395cc577a1c441e3ca201619e13deda73c8dc3.svg)\n\nSince the plate has uniform density, every unit of area contains the same amount of mass. Therefore, the mass density is constant and is given by the total mass divided by the total area:\n![](images/4e/4ea37907521bd2d810e88505cf91e44b78cd2400bba8ab67ef244c1cb62bdeaa.svg)\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis is\n\n![](images/ab/ab2f126d6507eb07fb23f834015c981794f3367a2f14b7e43ac688d5a9af803c.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129031 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8543" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129031 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/46/46d6a57700d1df7f2de9a46507821a8553876eb956be6ef87a455d5ca0fabafb.png)\n\nA plate occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the curves $y=x^2$ and $y=1$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. If the mass density function of $D$ is given by $\\lambda(x,y) = 4xy,$ find the moment of inertia of the lamina about the $x$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129031 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bee275c6-cca6-515f-b737-597ecca278a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129031 #edb/ref 1074 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda(x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nI_x =  \\iint\\limits_D y^2\\lambda(x,y)  \\:\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nI_y =  \\iint\\limits_D x^2\\lambda(x,y)  \\:\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\nI_0 = \\iint\\limits_D (x^2+y^2) \\lambda(x,y)  \\:\\textrm{d}A = I_x + I_y.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: x^2 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\right\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/88/8846da0fe3b1e8369a9277bbaba1e082d040136a71105a5816af7b13d2788027.png)\n\nUsing the formula for $I_x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}{y}^{2}\\cdot 4xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}4x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[x{y}^{4}]_{y={x}^{2}}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(1-{x}^{8})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x-{x}^{9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{10}}{10}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129211 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129211 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129211 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129211 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281632 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129211 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129211 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129211 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129211 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186367853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129211 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186367860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129211 #edb/ref 1