6129285 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186367867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129285 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129285 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129285 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129285 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating Moments of Inertia Using Polar Coordinates" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129285 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129285 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "eba1f811-7b7d-56f6-8b49-4e7a4c7db81b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129369 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130105 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129369 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129369 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129369 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130239 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129369 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129369 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129369 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129369 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129369 #edb/ref 1045 "Moments of Inertia of Laminas About the Coordinate Axes" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129369 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186130105 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129369 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129369 #edb/ref 1050 1440.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129369 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "5df21424-1e37-5c52-9fa1-bd4df936b142" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129532 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129532 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162906" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129532 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129532 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/64/64cadaa8b079c80659d661be55709e572c05b185a6b6fdb60e24801a9d1e84a5.png)\n\nA triangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region $D$ enclosed between the $x$-axis and the lines $y=2x$ and $y=3-x,$ as shown above. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $x$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129532 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f0af98bb-12cf-5c0f-a924-a0fe039adf99" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129532 #edb/ref 1074 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type II region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le 3-y\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/bc/bcf045b47856148dc5f7c05a2800804fb34aad2d0755983375c20c35bb00f0e4.png)\n\nThe area of the triangular plate $D$ is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 2=3.\n$$\n\nSince the plate has uniform density, every unit of area contains the same amount of mass. Therefore, the mass density is constant and is given by the total mass divided by the total area:\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y/2}^{3-y}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}[{\\int }_{y/2}^{3-y}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}(3-y-\\frac{1}{2}y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{[{y}^{3}-\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}[(8-6)-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129534 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126701 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129534 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162924" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129534 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129534 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ff/ff6c3e308d89c9d07cff993f097fe2fcfafb22dab3399b9056f8db167b1b4b80.png)\n\nA plate occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the curves $y=2x$ and $y={x}^{2}$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. If the mass density function of $D$ is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=6x+14y,$ find the moment of inertia of the lamina about the $y$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129534 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cea62eec-0c97-53ea-9e5d-d067ab50729f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129534 #edb/ref 1074 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 2x\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/fb/fb20e4c3052e28aeb2e6dad5054b0458db2d3b23d10991cfae27b5b00655a8fe.png)\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2x}{x}^{2}(6x+14y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2x}6{x}^{3}+14{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[6{x}^{3}y+7{x}^{2}{y}^{2}]_{y={x}^{2}}^{y=2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(12{x}^{4}+28{x}^{4})-(6{x}^{5}+7{x}^{6})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}40{x}^{4}-6{x}^{5}-7{x}^{6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[8{x}^{5}-{x}^{6}-{x}^{7}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =256-64-128 \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129535 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129535 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162986" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129535 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129535 #edb/ref 1071 "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\}.$\nThe mass density at each point is proportional to the square of its distance from the $y$-axis.\nFind the moment of inertia of the lamina about the origin." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129535 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "54c0ec35-7150-5d58-acd5-421129a7e7ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129535 #edb/ref 1074 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as $\\lambda (x,y)=k{x}^{2},$ where $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find an expression for $k$ in terms of $M$ by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}k{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =k({\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =k{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot [y]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =k(\\frac{1}{3}-0)(3-0) \\\\  & =k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=M$ and hence $\\lambda (x,y)=k{x}^{2}=M{x}^{2}.$\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =M{[\\frac{1}{5}y+\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =M(\\frac{3}{5}+3-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{18M}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129537 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129537 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162988" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129537 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129537 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/82/82ed1d9c8965c445488256f2319bd2e1a25b2f685d5ad39c88f2b2ba7f7f5525.png)\n\nConsider a plate of mass $M$ that occupies the region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ shown above. Given that the mass is distributed uniformly across the plate, find the moment of inertia of the plate about the origin." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129537 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f503a261-8e11-5e60-9902-aca301725e95" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129537 #edb/ref 1074 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ in polar coordinates can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the plate $D$ is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2}=\\frac{9\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the mass is distributed uniformly, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{4M}{9\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{9\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{9\\pi }({\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{9\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}{|}_{0}^{3}\\cdot \\theta {|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{9\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(81-0)\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9M}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129538 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129538 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162990" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129538 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129538 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/0a/0a85b0be5f8e5db2cf4d1716e4afe4a2780c500d94a1ce282a9d0044f93ba084.png)\n\nConsider the plate that occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the polar circles $r=1$ and $r=\\sqrt{3}$ and the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. The mass density function of the plate is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{6}{x}.$ Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129538 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "41c034f9-14b4-5bc4-9351-1214f69c2032" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129538 #edb/ref 1074 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that the region $D$ in polar coordinates can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nAlso, the mass density function in polar coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{6}{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{6}{r}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}(\\frac{6}{r}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ){r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}6{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =({\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}6{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =2{r}^{3}{|}_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =2(3\\sqrt{3}-1)\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1}{2}) \\\\  & =(3\\sqrt{3}-1)\\cdot (\\sqrt{3}-1) \\\\  & =10-4\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130105 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130105 #edb/ref 1061 31761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130105 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3933d4e7-a615-5065-8581-94f59b08025e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130105 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130411 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130106 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130239 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130106 #edb/ref 1061 31762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130106 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "07bfb470-2113-587d-b8ac-9dfa4cc8061f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130106 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130107 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130107 #edb/ref 1061 31765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130107 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a6003fb3-076a-5d38-ad49-10afc3a9a590" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130107 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129212 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130239 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130239 #edb/ref 1061 44944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130239 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1407188f-cbba-5d61-9842-deda3b5d7b07" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130239 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130240 #edb/ref 1061 44945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130240 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "83defff2-7dee-5a52-871f-690f395d1ba1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130240 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129285 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130241 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130241 #edb/ref 1061 44952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130241 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "25de40ad-47b7-51a1-b7b0-6a81e5b10517" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130241 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130252 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130252 #edb/ref 1061 45104 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130252 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3f899894-d69b-5df5-8333-4e2b804563ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130252 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130411 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose a massless, rigid lamina rotates with angular velocity $\\omega$ about a fixed axis $L.$ Attached to the lamina are a set of particles $P_1,P_2,\\ldots,P_n$ of masses $m_1,m_2,\\ldots,m_n$ at distances $r_1,r_2,\\ldots,r_n$ from the axis, respectively. \n\n![](images/b4/b4eeef756faf65e7caaf5bb8dad8d803cacf3e4f17b719868b647bf7e9c9da89.png)\n\nLet's find the total kinetic energy of this system.\n\nEach particle $P_i$ of mass $m_i$ has linear speed $v_i = r_i\\omega.$ So, its kinetic energy is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12m_iv_i^2 = \\dfrac12m_i(r_i\\omega)^2 = \\dfrac12m_ir_i^2\\omega^2.\n$$\n\nThe total kinetic energy of the system is obtained by adding the energies of all the particles. So, the kinetic energy of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total KE} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{2}{m}_{i}{r}_{i}^{2}{\\omega }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{r}_{i}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the total kinetic energy of the system is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Total KE} = \\dfrac12I\\omega^2,\n$$\n\nwhere the quantity\n\n$$\nI = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n m_ir_i^2\n$$\n\nis called the **moment of inertia** of the system about the fixed axis $L.$ The units of moment of inertia are $\\rm{kg}\\cdot\\rm{m}^2.$\n\nThe moment of inertia in angular motion is analogous to mass in linear motion:\n\n- For linear motion, the kinetic energy is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{KE} = \\dfrac12 \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{M} v^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the (total) mass of a system of particles.\n\nThe mass of a body measures how resistant the body is to changes in linear motion. It takes a larger force to induce a fixed change in linear motion for a large mass than for a smaller mass.\n\n- For angular motion, the kinetic energy is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{KE} = \\dfrac12 \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{I} \\omega^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $I$ is the moment of inertia of a system of particles.\n\nThe moment of inertia of a body measures how resistant the body is to changes in *angular* motion. It takes a larger force to induce a fixed change in angular motion for an object with a large moment of inertia than for a body with a smaller moment of inertia." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130411 #edb/ref 1065 5071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130411 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8d36ccdb-eb7d-55de-80e6-f75752dae9db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130411 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130472 #edb/ref 1064 "It's often convenient to calculate moments of inertia by changing from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. \n\nTo express an integral using polar coordinates, we use the change of variables formula given by\n\n![](images/fd/fdaacab5507fce09c2a1d31fe7c1aa46e80fab4c28db34574b6f214a5d4428e6.svg)\n\nApplying this formula to the moment of inertia about the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis of a plane lamina ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) with mass density function ![](images/ac/ac8c3fda52a02808979c7543290abb75797c85b3b0db8aed7a51cc2afda66995.svg) we get\n\n![](images/7d/7d3835a23ea01d7d6cab55dd71799f2b77c66f58456bdcf2b2968e9b9f09f175.svg)\n\nIf we repeat this process for moments of inertia about the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis and the origin, we obtain two more definitions. Let's summarize them below:\n\n- The moment of inertia of ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) about ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis is given by\n\n![](images/7c/7c2ed4b4705bdacaaf8c97d19a1bbf0b6ae56fe297dca5e5bdbe3c84094a1bcc.svg)\n\n- The moment of inertia of ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) about ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis is given by\n\n![](images/17/17efc2618db89a57e67423004eba2485ca6af8cd9f71fa271f9540cd0e3c8d74.svg)\n\n- The moment of inertia of ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) about origin is given by\n\n![](images/8f/8f79b08c632cff0162fb8f4c962d44c105e34eb32e8a11cf4eca3868e0362738.svg)\n\nNote that we denote ![](images/10/10b9e6b5ce99ae6bfc544d9480ac6d62cfa9d1f23f78b8b3a331c6c517e1472d.svg) the density function expressed in polar coordinates." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130472 #edb/ref 1065 7766 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130472 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5d1a8a74-d440-56ad-b42b-a46e22861e20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130472 #edb/ref 1067 "Moments of Inertia in Polar Coordinates" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130474 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's now find a formula for the moment of inertia of a plane lamina with continuously varying mass density.\n\nConsider a plane lamina that occupies the region $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2$ with mass density function $\\lambda(x,y),$ (in mass/area), which depends on the position.\n\nBy choosing a finite number of points $(x_{ij},y_{ij}),$ where $i=1,2,\\ldots,m$ and $j=1,2,\\ldots,n,$ we can break the region $D$ into non-overlapping boxes $B_{ij} = [x_{ij}, x_{ij} + \\Delta x] \\times [y_{ij}, y_{ij} + \\Delta y]$ of area $\\Delta x \\Delta y = \\Delta A.$\n\n![](images/c5/c5ac4166ed637b4de525f069673cc6abfaeb2e8e659b3a212d6f2f4d1bf9fc11.png)\n\nFor large $m$ and $n,$ the mass density in each box, $B_{ij}$ is approximately constant and equal to the mass density of a point $(x_{ij}^*, y_{ij}^*)$ in the box. Hence, the mass of this part of the lamina is approximately the product of the mass density at this point and the area of the box:\n\n$$\nm_{ij} \\approx \\lambda(x_{ij}^*, y_{ij}^*) \\cdot \\Delta A\n$$\n\nSuppose the function $r(x,y)$ gives the distance of each point $(x,y) \\in D$ from a fixed axis $L.$ Then, using the formula for the moment of inertia for a collection of masses, summing over these points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{i}[r({x}_{ij}^{∗},{y}_{ij}^{∗})]^{2} \\\\  & ≈{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(\\lambda ({x}_{ij}^{∗},{y}_{ij}^{∗})\\cdot ΔA)[r({x}_{ij}^{∗},{y}_{ij}^{∗})]^{2} \\\\  & ≈{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\lambda ({x}_{ij}^{∗},{y}_{ij}^{∗})[r({x}_{ij}^{∗},{y}_{ij}^{∗})]^{2}ΔA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the limit as the length and width of the boxes tend to zero, we get an exact representation of the moment of inertia as an integral:\n\n$$\nI = \\lim\\limits_{m,n\\to \\infty} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^m \\sum\\limits_{j=1}^n \\lambda(x_{ij}^*, y_{ij}^*) [r(x_{ij}^*, y_{ij}^*)]^2 \\Delta A\n= \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda(x,y) [r(x,y)]^2 \\:\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about a fixed axis $L$ of a plane lamina occupying the region $D$ with mass density $\\lambda(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nI_L = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda(x,y) [r(x,y)]^2 \\:\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the point $(x,y)$ to the axis $L.$\n\nIn this lesson, we'll limit the axis of rotation to the $x$- and $y$-axes. Note that the distance between a point $(x,y)$ and the $x$ and $y$ coordinate axes are $r_x(x,y) = |y|$ and $r_y(x,y) = |x|,$ respectively.\n\n![](images/8a/8a94fcdb8bb1a97e2bc9122e88ca7bd4f441299d2ce3c7bee819e644cf4eae89.png)\n\nHence, the moments of inertia of a plate with respect to the coordinate axes $x$ and $y,$ denoted $I_x$ and $I_y$, respectively, are \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\ {I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA related concept is the **moment of inertia about the origin** (or **polar moment of inertia**), which deals with the strength of an object when it is rotating. It is defined as\n\n$$\nI_0 = \\iint\\limits_D (x^2+y^2)\\lambda(x,y) \\:\\textrm dA = I_x + I_y.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130474 #edb/ref 1065 7794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130474 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "170c6cfe-d50a-5cee-b724-5409ca4c8461" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130474 #edb/ref 1067 "Moment of Inertia of Plane Laminas" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278732 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{20M}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278732 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278732 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0fe52c0a-9e8d-5b4f-99c3-86f55225cf03" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278741 #edb/ref 1080 "6M\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278741 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278741 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "10a700cc-9a80-52fe-b948-d035b0b0b50c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279066 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{10}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279066 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279066 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "365b10c1-9a22-5c79-b9b5-50173e8aadde" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279374 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{18M}{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279374 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279374 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "558e7a6e-54fa-5cf5-b950-f4588987db71" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279596 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{15M}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279596 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279596 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6e7a5abc-84ff-5f6c-a768-c87b8daaf933" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279760 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{16M}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279760 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279760 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8278e096-7229-5cc7-8e98-ca881cad0f81" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279906 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{8M}{9}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186